# 09_aec.patch   (2.17 port - replaces the dead 2.12 09_aec.patch)
#
# WebRTC AEC fixes: super-wideband band splitting, thread safe reset,
# resync API, early delay correction, capture high pass filter.
#
# Background
# ----------
# From 2013 until the pjsip 2.10 migration (May 2021) sipsimple shipped its
# own WebRTC AEC wrapper, pjmedia/src/pjmedia/echo_webrtc_aec.c (Saul Ibarra
# / Dan Pascu, darcs history 2013-06..2013-07), built from
# pjmedia/build/os-auto.mak.in with "PJMEDIA_OBJS += echo_webrtc_aec.o" and
# its own copy of the WebRTC sources.  When deps/pjsip was replaced by the
# pjproject tarball + patch series, the file survived in the monolithic
# 001_pjsip_210.patch (later split into 09_aec.patch) but the build line did
# not.  Every 2.10, 2.11, 2.12 and 2.17 build since then has linked pjsip's
# upstream echo_webrtc.c instead (verified with strings(1) on the shipped
# _core*.so: only echo_webrtc.o, never echo_webrtc_aec.o).  The old wrapper
# could not have been revived as-is: it targets the 2011 WebRTC API.
#
# The upstream wrapper has the problems the 2013 wrapper had solved, plus
# one that neither addressed:
#
#  1. 32 kHz (Blink's sample rate whenever EC is on) is processed wrongly.
#     echo_webrtc.c feeds 160 full band samples (5 ms) with num_bands=1 to a
#     core initialized for 32 kHz, which expects the QMF-split low band plus
#     the high band (num_bands=2).  The core runs the 32 kHz signal as if it
#     was 16 kHz: half the filter length, half the delay estimator range,
#     mis-scaled NLP.  aec_core.c asserts on this; the assert is compiled out
#     by -DNDEBUG.  pjsip issue #2404, closed as wontfix.  48 kHz is worse.
#     The 2013 wrapper split bands with WebRtcSpl_AnalysisQMF (darcs:
#     "pjsip: Fixed echo cancelling when using 32kHz sample rate").
#
#  2. pjmedia_echo_reset() is not thread safe.  sipsimple calls
#     pjmedia_snd_port_reset_ec_state() from the Python thread on every
#     stream start and direction change, and patch 08 re-enabled the reset in
#     sound_port.c play_cb when EC resumes after 5 s of silence (upstream had
#     it commented out).  The reset moves frames between the latency lists,
#     clears lat_ready and re-inits the WebRTC core while the capture thread
#     is using all of them.  With a stress test (playback/capture threads +
#     reset thread) the unpatched code segfaults in 4 of 25 runs at 16 kHz,
#     and in other runs the canceller stops processing for the rest of the
#     stream (corrupted latency list).  The 2013 wrapper deferred
#     the reset to the processing thread (darcs: "pjsip: Fixed resetting AEC
#     state").
#
#  3. Every reset throws away the learned echo path, and the WebRTC core does
#     not apply its signal based delay correction until 1500 frames (15 s)
#     after init.  pjsip does not report the real system delay (sipsimple
#     passes tail_length=2 ms), so whenever the acoustic delay is not already
#     inside the filter window (external/Bluetooth speakers, HDMI audio,
#     > ~60 ms) no echo is cancelled during the first 15 s of each call and
#     for 15 s after every reset / EC resume.
#
# What this patch changes
# -----------------------
# pjmedia/src/pjmedia/echo_webrtc.c (desktop path; AECM mobile path unchanged)
#   - Processes 10 ms blocks at the core rate.
#   - 32 kHz: near and far end split with WebRtcSpl_AnalysisQMF, core runs
#     with num_bands=2, output recombined with WebRtcSpl_SynthesisQMF.
#   - Other rates (48 kHz, 44.1 kHz, ...): near and far end resampled to
#     32 kHz (16 kHz for rates below 16 kHz) with pjmedia_resample, output
#     resampled back.  Content above 16 kHz on the mic path is dropped.
#   - Frames must be a whole number of 10 ms blocks (PJ_ENOTSUP otherwise).
#   - 2nd order ~80 Hz high pass filter on the capture low band before AEC,
#     as webrtc::AudioProcessing and the 2013 wrapper do.
#   - Noise suppressor (PJMEDIA_ECHO_USE_NOISE_SUPPRESSOR) band aware; NS
#     analysis on the raw capture, processing after the AEC.
#   - Saturating float->int16 conversion.
#   - Statistics: the capture thread snapshots WebRtcAec_GetDelayMetrics()
#     and WebRtcAec_GetMetrics() once per second; webrtc_aec_get_stat()
#     (pjmedia_echo_get_stat / pjmedia_snd_port_get_ec_stat) copies the
#     snapshot under a sequence counter and fills delay, std, frac_delay,
#     return_loss (ERL), return_loss_enh (ERLE), duration and stat_info
#     ("delay=20ms std=0ms poor=0.00 ERL=6dB ERLE=23dB (now 26dB) NLP=8dB
#     rate=32000/32000 9s"; values WebRTC has not measured yet print as
#     "n/a").  Returns PJ_EPENDING during the first second.
#     Upstream called the WebRTC metric functions from the caller's thread,
#     racing the capture thread (UpdateDelayMetrics() clears the histogram).
#
# pjmedia/src/pjmedia/echo_common.c, pjmedia/include/pjmedia/echo.h
#   - pjmedia_echo_reset() only raises a flag; the reset is carried out by
#     the capture thread (pjmedia_echo_capture(), or pjmedia_echo_cancel()
#     for users without latency buffer) before the next frame.  Ownership of
#     the latency list is handed over through lat_ready exactly like the
#     existing prefetch logic, so no lock is taken in the audio path.
#   - New pjmedia_echo_resync(): same deferral, but only flushes and
#     re-primes the latency/delay buffers and keeps the adaptive filter and
#     delay estimate.
#
# pjmedia/src/pjmedia/sound_port.c, pjmedia/include/pjmedia/sound_port.h
#   - play_cb: EC resume after inactivity uses pjmedia_echo_resync() instead
#     of a full reset (the acoustic path did not change while idle).
#   - New pjmedia_snd_port_resync_ec_state(), advertised with
#     #define PJMEDIA_HAS_SND_PORT_RESYNC_EC 1 so bindings can detect it.
#   - pjmedia_snd_port_reset_ec_state() reads ec_state once (no double read).
#
# third_party/webrtc/src/webrtc/modules/audio_processing/aec/aec_core.c
#   - kDelayCorrectionStart 1500 -> 200 frames (2 s), overridable with
#     -DWEBRTC_AEC_DELAY_CORRECTION_START=N.  The delay quality threshold
#     still gates corrections; silent / -35 dB far end start, mute periods
#     and headset (no echo) cases were tested for regressions.
#
# third_party/build/webrtc/Makefile, third_party/webrtc/CMakeLists.txt
#   - Build common_audio/signal_processing/splitting_filter.c (QMF bank).
#     The MSVC project files already list it.
#
# Measurements (offline harness, pjsip speech vectors, simulated room with
# 60 ms / 150 ms delay and 150-300 ms reverb, sipsimple parameters: 20 ms
# frames, tail 2 ms, latency 20 ms).  ERLE in dB per 2 s window:
#
#   32 kHz, 60 ms   before:  8 11 11  5 19 35 35 34 35 35 ... DT 12 35 34 34
#                   after : 14 27 28 30 29 29 29 28 29 28 ... DT 27 28 28 28
#   32 kHz, 150 ms  before:  3  2  3  1 18 32 30 31 14 33 12  8 DT  8 14  9  8
#                   after :  3  5 28 29 29 28 29 28 28 28 28 27 DT 26 28 27 28
#   48 kHz, 150 ms  before:  3  2  4 14 18 34 12  9  6 17 13 20 DT  8 20 11 22
#                   after :  7 17 30 32 31 31 31 31 31 31 31 31 DT 29 30 30 30
#   16 kHz, 150 ms  before:  3  5  5  3  4  2  1  3 26 25 25 24 DT 23 23 23 23
#                   after :  3 13 26 27 26 26 27 25 27 24 27 25 DT 25 25 26 24
#
# With PJMEDIA_ECHO_USE_NOISE_SUPPRESSOR steady state ERLE is ~6 dB higher.
# Near end speech is attenuated ~7 dB during double talk at 16 and 32 kHz
# (before: 7 dB at 16 kHz, 3.6 dB at 32 kHz, where echo also leaked).
#
# Reset stress test (playback + capture + reset/resync threads):
#   before: 16 kHz segfault 4/25 runs, some runs stop cancelling for good
#   after : 0/75 failures at 16/32/48 kHz, ERLE back to 28-29 dB in 2-4 s
#
# See docs/EchoCancellation.txt for the full write-up and the harness.
#
